{"name":"White Hydrangea","hex":"#f9f6dd","fetched":"2026-10-13","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"white","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Bright and colourless; reflecting equal quantities of all frequencies of visible light.","Of or relating to Europeans or those of European descent, regardless if their skin has cool or warm undertones."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The color of snow or milk; the color of light containing equal amounts of all visible wavelengths.","A person of European descent with light-colored skin."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/white"},{"term":"hydrangea","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Any of several shrubs, of the genus Hydrangea, having large clusters of white, pink or blue flowers."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/hydrangea"}],"entries":[{"title":"Hydrangea","blocks":[{"text":"Hydrangea is a genus of more than 70 species of flowering plants native to Asia and the Americas. Hydrangea is also used as the common name for the genus; some are also often called hortensia. The genus was first described from Virginia in North America, but by far the greatest species diversity is in eastern Asia, notably China, Korea, and Japan. Most are shrubs 1–3 m tall, but some are small trees, and others lianas reaching up to 30 m (100 ft) by climbing up trees. They can be either deciduous or evergreen, though the widely cultivated temperate species are all deciduous."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/fc/Hydrangea_arborescens_139866012.jpg/330px-Hydrangea_arborescens_139866012.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hydrangea"},{"title":"White","blocks":[{"text":"White is the lightest color and is achromatic. It is the color of objects such as snow, chalk, and milk, and is the opposite of black. White objects fully reflect and scatter all the visible wavelengths of light. White on television and computer screens is created by a mixture of red, blue, and green light. The color white can be given with white pigments, especially titanium dioxide."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e2/Delphinapterus_leucas_2.jpg/330px-Delphinapterus_leucas_2.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/White"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a9/Hydrangea_Flower_Color_Based_on_Soil_pH.jpg/500px-Hydrangea_Flower_Color_Based_on_Soil_pH.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a9/Hydrangea_Flower_Color_Based_on_Soil_pH.jpg/500px-Hydrangea_Flower_Color_Based_on_Soil_pH.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Hydrangea flower color changes based on the pH in soil. As the graph depicts, soil with a pH of 5.5 or lower will produce blue flowers, a pH of 6.5 or higher will produce pink hydrangeas, and soil in between 5.5 and 6.5 will have purple hydrangeas.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AHydrangea_Flower_Color_Based_on_Soil_pH.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1c/Hydrangea_paniculata_-_flower_view_01.jpg/500px-Hydrangea_paniculata_-_flower_view_01.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1c/Hydrangea_paniculata_-_flower_view_01.jpg/500px-Hydrangea_paniculata_-_flower_view_01.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Hydrangea paniculata","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AHydrangea_paniculata_-_flower_view_01.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d8/Hydrangea_knowltoni_as_Hydrangea_bendirei_USNM_P36979_img3.jpg/330px-Hydrangea_knowltoni_as_Hydrangea_bendirei_USNM_P36979_img3.